/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (The auto_solve_bfs method starts by looping continuously while the grid is not uniform, meaning not all cells have the same color. Inside the loop, it calculates the number of neighboring cells for each color using get_neighbors_color_counts\(\). It then selects the color with the highest count using the max function. Once the best color is chosen, it performs a BFS flood fill starting from the top-left cell \(0, 0\) using that color, which changes connected areas to match the selected color.)
/E (The auto_solve_bfs method starts by looping continuously while the grid is not uniform, meaning not all cells have the same color. Inside the loop, it calculates the number of neighboring cells for each color using get_neighbors_color_counts\(\). It then selects the color with the highest count using the max function. Once the best color is chosen, it performs a BFS flood fill starting from the top-left cell \(0, 0\) using that color, which changes connected areas to match the selected color.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
255 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (After that, it redraws the updated grid on the canvas with draw_grid\(\), updates the UI immediately with master.update\(\), and pauses for 0.3 seconds using time.sleep\(0.3\) to allow the user to see the changes. The auto_solve_dfs method works in exactly the same way, except it uses DFS \(flood_fill_dfs\) instead of BFS to apply the color change. Finally, the reset_grid method reinitializes the entire grid by filling it with random colors using nested list comprehensions, and then draws the new random grid using draw_grid\(\).)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
